/* ============================================================================
population-variance.epher -- variance of the ten scores, dividing by n

The variance of the exam scores in `d`, from the builtin variance and
from its defining formula, the mean squared deviation from the mean.
epher's variance is the POPULATION variance: it divides by the count n
(guide 1.20). When the data are a sample, use the n - 1 version instead
(sample-variance.epher shows it).

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/statistics/summary/population-variance.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\statistics\summary\population-variance.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/statistics/summary/population-variance.epher
In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
Everything also works line by line in the TUI.

The one knob to edit is `d` below. Everything above the results section
is machinery you can leave alone.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob -----------------------------------------------------------------
const d = {72, 85, 91, 64, 88, 79, 83, 95, 70, 86}   // exam scores, 10 students

// ---- population variance: mean squared deviation, divide by n -------------------
variance(d)                         // builtin variance, population version (n)
sum((d - mean(d)) ^ 2) / len(d)     // the formula: sum of squared deviations over n
sum((d - mean(d)) ^ 2)              // the squared deviations total 884.1

/* ---- expected output ----
= {72, 85, 91, 64, 88, 79, 83, 95, 70, 86}
= 88.41
= 88.41
= 884.1
*/